'''RapidAir''' is a [[Balboa|Balboan]] [[low-cost carrier]] headquartered in [[Portnou]], [[Estret Province]], [[Balboa]]. It is the second-largest airline in Balboa after the flag carrier [[Aeroístmus]], operating domestic routes from its hubs at [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]] and [[Cecilio Ríos International Airport]], as well as short-haul international services. The airline was founded in 1984 as a small regional carrier during the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|Balboa real-estate bubble]] and transitioned to a low-cost model following the [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] of the late 1980s.

== History ==

=== Founding and early operations (1984–1991) ===

RapidAir was founded in 1984 by Jordi Mas i Vidal (b. c. 1945), a [[Portnou]] shipping and transport entrepreneur who recognised that the [[Balboa real-estate bubble|Balboa real-estate bubble]] was generating demand for air travel within the Estret corridor. The airline was headquartered in Portnou but initially operated from [[Ciutat de l'Estació Airport|Ciutat de l'Estació Airport]] — the principal airport serving Sant Cristòfor at the time — using a single {{w|Boeing 737-200|Boeing 737-200}} on charter and scheduled services connecting Sant Cristòfor to [[Portnou]] and [[Sant Bart]]. The business model was similar to that of [[AeroTerminus]] in Illa Daurada: serving the affluent clientele created by the real-estate boom — developers, investors, and property owners who required frequent travel between the canal corridor's principal towns.

When [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]] opened in Sant Martí in 1985, the old Ciutat de l'Estació airport was redesignated [[Areza International Airport]], dedicated to serving the [[International City of Areza]]. RapidAir relocated its Sant Cristòfor operations to the new SCI airport, while retaining its headquarters in Portnou.

The [[Balboa real-estate bubble|real-estate bubble]] burst in 1987, and the subsequent [[Balboa real-estate asset crisis]] (1985–1991) eliminated the wealthy clientele on which RapidAir, like AeroTerminus, depended. Unlike AeroTerminus, which went bankrupt, RapidAir survived the crisis by pivoting to a low-cost carrier model. The airline recognised that the post-crisis Balboan market offered a different opportunity: a growing middle class that could not afford Aeroístmus's full-service fares but would travel if prices were low enough.

=== Transition to low-cost carrier (1991–2003) ===

The transition to a low-cost model began in the early 1990s. RapidAir adopted the standard LCC practices of the period: single-class seating, buy-on-board food and beverage service, no free checked baggage, and aggressive fare competition with [[Aeroístmus]] on high-traffic domestic routes. The airline expanded its route network beyond the Estret corridor, adding services from [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport|Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Cecilio Ríos International Airport|Sant Bart]] to destinations across Balboa.

When [[Portnou International Airport]] opened in 1992, RapidAir moved its principal operations from Sant Cristòfor to the new airport, which was located in the airline's home city. The move gave RapidAir a strategic advantage on routes serving the southern Estret corridor, as it could offer direct departures from Portnou without the congestion and higher landing fees of SCI. The airline became one of the launch carriers at the new airport, operating domestic services alongside [[Aeroístmus]]. The airline's fleet of Boeing 737s was supplemented by {{w|ATR 72}} turboprops for thinner routes to airports that could not justify jet service or could not accommodate jet aircraft, such as [[Costabella Airport|Costabella]].

On 3 September 1993, a RapidAir Boeing 737 was involved in a [[runway incursion]] at [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport]] (see [[#Accidents and incidents|Accidents and incidents]]). The incident prompted the [[Balboa Airports Authority]] to implement mandatory readback procedures for all runway entry clearances.

=== International services (2003–present) ===

In 2003, RapidAir introduced its first international services from [[Portnou International Airport|Portnou]], offering flights to [[Tamsui International Airport|Tamsui]] in [[Sinchew]] ([[Lurandia]]) and [[Gran Port de Sant Mateu International Airport|Gran Port de Sant Mateu]] in [[Tapuya]] ([[Abyala]]). The international routes reflected the growing economic links between the Portnou port region and destinations across the Shendan Ocean, and the increasing demand for direct international connections from the southern Estret corridor without transiting Sant Cristòfor.

In 2013, RapidAir undertook a major fleet renewal, replacing its ageing Boeing 737 fleet with the {{w|Airbus A320 family|Airbus A320}} family. The transition to A320s reduced fuel costs and increased capacity on the airline's busiest routes, and marked the completion of the airline's two-decade transformation from a small charter operator to a modern low-cost carrier.

In 2023, RapidAir expanded its international network beyond Portnou, launching a service from [[Sant Cristòfor International Airport|Sant Cristòfor]] to [[Tamsui International Airport|Tamsui]]. The route was the airline's first international service from SCI and placed it in direct competition with [[Aeroístmus]] and [[Sinchew Air]] on the Sant Cristòfor–Tamsui corridor, one of the busiest international routes in the isthmus region.

